RATIONALE:
  Unanimous 5/5 pick for Charter A over Charter C. Charter C is killed on a mechanism error that no
  reviewer rebutted: production prefix caches (RadixAttention 2312.07104, vLLM-APC 2309.06180) are
  token-content-addressed, so changed file content yields a miss, not a stale hit — collapsing C's
  "correctness ceiling" thesis into a strawman, compounded by a degenerate Codex LB95=1.000 and
  collision with PROJ-0002/DEAD-0016.

  Charter A survives with two YELLOW concerns, both fixable at pre-registration: (1) "serving dollar"
  overclaim from a token-mass measurement (theory_skeptic — fix: reframe to "token mass," add optional
  cost-weighted secondary); (2) CC LB95=0.502 borderline near the 0.50 gate (product_realist,
  theory_skeptic — fix: cross-instrument equivalence margin + the Codex LB95=0.845 provides the
  strong-instrument anchor). Neither threatens viability.

  The contribution — first granular token-mass decomposition (tool-result 63.3%/86.1%, Gini 0.864/0.746,
  top-decile 78.8%/57.2%) of real agent serving traces, replicating cross-instrument — is cleanly
  separated from all cited prior work (DistServe, SplitWise, Parrot, Autellix, RadixAttention, vLLM-APC,
  Sarathi-Serve) which implement disaggregation/caching knobs but ASSUME the workload mix rather than
  measuring it. The heavy-tail finding (Gini 0.864) is the genuinely novel delta beyond "prefill-heavy"
  folklore, identifying cacheable hot spots. Pre-measured, killable (LB95<0.50 OR Gini<0.5 = clean
  negative), and able to produce a publishable positive (the headline numbers ARE the contribution).

  GREEN conditioned on L0 pre-registration honoring all 9 mandatory fixes above. (v001)
